1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A program spends nearly all its time in a few loops, so the optimiser spends nearly all its effort there.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Here is the loop this page rewrites: an index scaled by four, an array read, a running sum, a counter and a test.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Four rewrites act on it: strength reduction, loop-invariant code motion, induction variable elimination and unrolling.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The ledger for n iterations starts at 2n multiplies and 3n adds — five arithmetic operations per iteration.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
t1 = 4 * i is a multiply whose right operand never changes. That is what makes it a candidate.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Set t1 = 0 before the loop and replace the multiply by t1 = t1 + 4. The eight values are identical.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The index multiplies fall from n to 0, and the adds rise from 3n to 4n. The arithmetic total does not move.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The rule: an induction variable is one that steps by a constant, and its affine use can always be replaced by a running total.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Two conditions licence the rewrite: the multiplier must be loop-invariant, and the stride of the induction variable must be constant.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Break the stride: if the body doubles i, the running add gives 0, 4, 8, 12, 16 where the multiply gives 0, 4, 8, 16, 32.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A multiplier that is not a power of two still works: multiplying by seven becomes adding seven.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
But x * 2 to x << 1 is not a legal rewrite in C when x can be negative: shifting a negative value is undefined.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Now the second rewrite. t3 = b * c sits in the body and produces the same value on every iteration.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Move it to the preheader, the block that runs once before the loop. The multiplies fall from 2n to n + 1.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Two conditions: every operand must be invariant, and the moved definition must reach every exit of the loop.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The saving is real but bounded: one multiply saved per iteration, and nothing else in the loop changes.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
First failure: an operand that the loop writes. Here the loop does x = x + 1, so x * x is not invariant.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Second failure: a loop that runs zero times. Hoisting 100 / y out of it executes a division that the original never executed.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The repair is a guard: hoist only if the loop is known to run at least once, or move the value into an if that tests the trip count.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
And a third cost, not a failure: the hoisted value stays live across the whole loop, so it may occupy a register for n iterations.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
After strength reduction, i is used for exactly two things: stepping t1, and the test i < n.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Rewrite the test as t1 < limit with limit = 4 * n in the preheader. Then i has no uses, and the increment is deleted.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The deleted increment is not dead code elimination done first: the rewrite is what makes i dead, and then the deletion is trivial.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The boundary: 4 * n can overflow where i < n could not, so the rewrite is not free at the edge of the machine word.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The fourth rewrite does not remove work. It removes tests: replicate the body four times and test once.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
For n equal to eight the tests fall from 8 to 2. For n equal to ten they fall from 10 to 4.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
A remainder loop is not optional: without it the last n mod 4 iterations are silently dropped.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The trade is body size against test count, and past a point the larger body costs more in the instruction cache than it saves.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Four versions, one ledger. Arithmetic operations for n iterations: 5n, then 4n + 1, 4n + 1, and 3n + 2.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Versions one and two reach the same total by different routes: one removes a multiply, the other turns it into an add.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Only the third rewrite removes an operation outright: the increment disappears with i, saving exactly n adds.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The loads and the tests never move: no rewrite here removes an array access or a branch except unrolling.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Shape one, from GATE CSE 2006: a loop is printed and four optimisations are named, and one of them has no scope.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
There is invariance, there is a repeated subexpression, there is a multiply to reduce, and there is no dead code — so the false option is dead code elimination.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Shape two, from GATE CSE 2021 Set 2: count the additions and the dereferences after common subexpression elimination.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Both shapes reward the same habit: count what is inside the loop, and check separately what moved outside it.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: find the invariants. Step two: find the induction variables and their strides.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step three: ask what the rewrite makes dead. Step four: ask what it makes unsafe.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
The five traps: instruction count against cost, zero-trip loops, naming the optimisations in the wrong order, overflow, and signed shifts.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
None of this is in the syllabus as a heading. It is what the word local, in local optimisation, is measured against.

